<DocsLayout title="Case Study: Black October" description="How PressHaven is exposed to and protected against PHP2Shell and Node2Shell, including deployments still running older PHP or Node inside their containers" section="AI-Era Defense" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    <strong>Black October</strong> (October 2026) is the first wave the rest of this section was written for: a
    cluster of critical, AI-accelerated disclosures in software almost every web stack runs, exploited within
    days. Two of them matter directly to a PressHaven deployment: <strong>PHP2Shell</strong> in the PHP runtime
    that every WordPress container runs, and <strong>Node2Shell</strong> in the Brotli decoder bundled with
    Node.js, which Kibana runs. This page states plainly what is exposed, what the existing layers do and don't
    cover, and what to change, including when an image can't be upgraded straight away.
  </p>

  <div class="admonition warning">
    <div class="admonition-title">Short version</div>
    <p>The shipped images are affected. Every WordPress container (production, every decoy pool and wp-cli)
    runs <code>wordpress:6.8.3-php8.1</code>, which is PHP 8.1.33 under Apache <code>mod_php</code>. PHP 8.1 is
    end of life and gets no binary fix. Kibana 8.17.3 bundles Node 20.18.2, which is also end of life and gets
    no Brotli fix. The edge (OpenResty) isn't affected. <a href="#now">Upgrade the images</a>. Until you can,
    apply the <a href="#old">interim PHP settings</a> and the <a href="#guard">body guard</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="summary">What Happened</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <DocCard title="PHP2Shell" icon="alert" tag="CVE-2026-109618 / 109619" description="Remote code execution in PHP 5.4–8.5's request-body handling, triggered before any application code runs. Fixed in 8.2.35, 8.3.36, 8.4.27 and 8.5.12. Only source backports exist for 7.4–8.1." meta="pre-auth RCE" />
    <DocCard title="Node2Shell" icon="alert" tag="CVE-2026-111497" description="Out-of-bounds write in the Brotli decoder bundled with Node, reachable through node:zlib Brotli decompression of attacker data. Fixed in 22.23.4, 24.22.1 and 26.12.1. No fix for Node 20 or 25." meta="decompression RCE" />
  </CardGrid>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>CVE-2026-109618.</strong> PHP runs out of memory while it parses <code>$_POST</code> or a
    multipart body. Deeply nested array field names push it to <code>memory_limit</code>, which leaves a hash
    bucket uninitialised. That bucket is visited again during shutdown, and the attacker controls what's in it.</li>
    <li><strong>CVE-2026-109619.</strong> Stale per-worker state survives into the next request on the same
    worker, giving a use-after-free. It can be reached through file uploads and session upload progress
    (<code>mod_php</code> and FPM alike, needs a reused worker). On 8.5 it can also be reached through
    <code>fatal_error_backtraces</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Exploitation.</strong> Every known trigger is a multipart request body. Exploitation was seen
    from 17 October, and watchTowr confirmed active attempts. A successful exploit can return
    <strong>HTTP 200</strong>, so status codes won't show it.</li>
    <li><strong>Node2Shell.</strong> No official workaround exists. The only interim advice is to stop handing
    untrusted Brotli data (<code>Content-Encoding: br</code>) to an affected Node process.</li>
    <li><strong>Around them.</strong> The same weeks brought the eMudhra CA compromise, a LiteLLM supply-chain
    attack (PyPI 1.107.0 and its Docker images) and a CVSS 10 GitLab RCE. None of these ship in PressHaven, but
    they shape the <a href="#supply">supply-chain lessons</a> below.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="exposure">Exposure in This Project</h2>
  <p>Checked against the running containers of the current compose files:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Component</th><th>Image / runtime</th><th>PHP2Shell</th><th>Node2Shell</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Edge (<code>openresty/openresty:alpine</code>)</td><td>OpenResty 1.31.1.1, OpenSSL 3.5.8</td><td>Not affected: no PHP</td><td>Not affected: no Node, no Brotli module, nginx doesn't decompress request bodies</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Production storefront</td><td><code>wordpress:6.8.3-php8.1</code>: PHP 8.1.33, Apache 2.4.65, <code>mod_php</code> prefork</td><td><strong>Vulnerable</strong>, EOL branch</td><td>n/a</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Decoy pools (<code>honeypot_eshop_N</code>)</td><td>Same image as production</td><td><strong>Vulnerable</strong></td><td>n/a</td></tr>
    <tr><td>wp-cli sidecars</td><td><code>wordpress:cli-php8.1</code></td><td>Vulnerable binary, but not reachable over HTTP</td><td>n/a</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Kibana (SIEM host)</td><td><code>kibana:8.17.3</code>, bundled Node 20.18.2</td><td>n/a</td><td><strong>Vulnerable runtime</strong>, EOL branch. Reachability: <a href="#kibana">see below</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pool manager, dashboard</td><td>Python 3.12</td><td>n/a</td><td>No Python <code>brotli</code> package installed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Database</td><td><code>mysql:5.7</code></td><td>n/a</td><td>n/a (EOL, but only on internal networks)</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>The stock PHP settings make it worse. <code>memory_limit=128M</code> is easy to reach while parsing, and
  <code>session.upload_progress.enabled=1</code> exposes the upload-progress path. <code>disable_functions</code>
  is empty, so a working exploit gets a shell immediately. Also, the edge keeps up to 512 idle keepalive
  connections to production. That means requests from different clients are served one after another by the
  same Apache worker, which is exactly the worker reuse the 109619 path needs.</p>

  <h2 id="already">What Already Helps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The edge parses nothing.</strong> Every request crosses OpenResty first. It has no PHP and no
    Brotli, so a malformed body can't hurt the proxy itself, and the edge is the right place to
    <a href="#guard">inspect that body</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Full-body buffering is the default.</strong> The config never turns off
    <code>proxy_request_buffering</code>, so nginx receives the whole body, chunked bodies included, before
    passing anything to PHP. A guard at the edge therefore sees the same bytes PHP will parse.</li>
    <li><strong>Behavioural diversion.</strong> Clients that probe first, run scanners, upload suspicious files
    or come from bad-reputation IPs are diverted to a pool <em>before</em> their exploit request, as described in
    <a href="/docs/ai-defense/deception#sensor">Deception</a>. Mass PHP2Shell scanning mostly comes from exactly
    those clients.</li>
    <li><strong>Upload scoring.</strong> <code>upload_rules.lua</code> already scores multipart requests and
    suspicious file names on the <code>admin-ajax.php</code> upload path.</li>
    <li><strong>Containment pieces.</strong> Every pool has its own database credentials. WordPress containers
    have <code>mem_limit</code> set. The SIEM sits on a separate host behind an outbound-only mTLS channel, so an
    exploited storefront can't erase its own trail.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="gaps">What Does Not Help</h2>
  <p>Being honest about these matters more than the list above:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Application-level controls are bypassed.</strong> The bug fires while PHP parses the body,
    before WordPress, its login, its nonces or any plugin runs. Admin gating, <code>DISALLOW_FILE_*</code> and
    the route manifest don't protect anything if the request still reaches PHP. (The manifest helps only if it
    also restricts which routes may receive multipart.)</li>
    <li><strong>First-request exploits aren't diverted.</strong> A single multipart POST from a clean IP to
    <code>/</code> has no signal for stages 3–10 to act on today. It goes straight to vulnerable production PHP.</li>
    <li><strong>Diversion sends the exploit to vulnerable PHP too.</strong> The pools run the same PHP, so a
    diverted PHP2Shell <em>succeeds</em> there. That's a real RCE inside a decoy on the flat pool network.
    The <a href="/docs/engine">Honeypot Engine</a> is designed to recover from that, but the recommendation
    below is to <a href="#quarantine">stop forwarding parser exploits to any PHP</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>The seeded CVE table doesn't match it.</strong> There's no fixed URL or parameter to sign. The
    shape lives in the body's structure, which no current rule parses.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="now">Do This Now</h2>
  <p>Patching is the fix. The rest of the page explains how to stay safe until it's done.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Move every WordPress service off PHP 8.1.</strong> In <code>ids/docker-compose.yml</code>,
    change <code>wordpress:6.8.3-php8.1</code> (production and every <code>honeypot_eshop_N</code>) and
    <code>wordpress:cli-php8.1</code> to a <code>-php8.3</code> or <code>-php8.4</code> tag whose PHP is at least
    8.3.36 or 8.4.27. Pin it by digest (<code>image: wordpress@sha256:…</code>) after checking
    <code>docker run --rm &lt;image&gt; php -v</code>. Roll it through a pool first, using the
    <a href="/docs/ai-defense/hardening#updates">pool-first path</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Upgrade Kibana.</strong> Raise <code>STACK_VERSION</code> in <code>siem/docker/.env</code> to an
    Elastic release whose bundled Node is at least 22.23.4. Check with
    <code>docker run --rm --entrypoint /usr/share/kibana/node/bin/node &lt;image&gt; -v</code>. Elasticsearch
    and Kibana must stay on the same version.</li>
    <li><strong>Apply the interim settings anyway.</strong> The <a href="#old">PHP settings</a> and the
    <a href="#guard">body guard</a> also cover the next parser bug, not just this one.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="old">If You Must Stay on Old PHP or Node</h2>
  <p>Sometimes an image can't move immediately: a plugin that breaks on 8.3, a change freeze, a Kibana upgrade
  waiting on Elasticsearch. These settings narrow the bug for both CVEs without a patched binary. Mount them
  into <strong>every</strong> WordPress container, pools included:</p>
  <pre><code>; /usr/local/etc/php/conf.d/zz-black-october.ini  (interim, PHP2Shell)
; 109618: the parser can no longer hit memory_limit mid-parse.
; The container mem_limit (1024m) is the real cap, so the worst case
; becomes an OOM-killed container (DoS) instead of a corrupted heap (RCE).
memory_limit = -1
; 109619: close the upload-progress path
session.upload_progress.enabled = 0
; Shrink what the parser has to build
max_input_vars = 200
max_input_nesting_level = 8
post_max_size = 8M
upload_max_filesize = 2M
max_file_uploads = 5
; Make a successful exploit worth less
disable_functions = exec,system,passthru,shell_exec,proc_open,popen,pcntl_exec
expose_php = Off</code></pre>
  <pre><code># /etc/apache2/conf-enabled/zz-black-october.conf  (interim)
# Stale per-worker state needs worker reuse: recycle prefork children often.
# Pools: 1 (one connection per child). Production: low, e.g. 50.
MaxConnectionsPerChild 50
LimitRequestBody 8388608
LimitRequestFieldSize 8190
LimitRequestFields 100</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Do not use <code>RLIMIT_AS</code> or <code>RLIMIT_DATA</code></strong> (<code>ulimit -v</code>)
    as the memory cap. Out-of-memory then happens inside the allocator, mid-parse, which brings back the state
    the fix is avoiding. Keep using Docker's cgroup <code>mem_limit</code>, which is already set.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the side effects.</strong> <code>memory_limit = -1</code> lets one request use the
    container's whole memory, and WordPress respects <code>-1</code>. Keep <code>mem_limit</code> and
    <code>pids_limit</code> on the container and the edge's rate limits in place. Lower
    <code>max_input_vars</code> only after confirming that no admin screen you use posts more fields than
    that. Large WooCommerce variation forms can.</li>
    <li><strong>Treat <code>MaxConnectionsPerChild</code> as a mitigation, not a fix.</strong> It shrinks the
    window for cross-request state but doesn't remove it within one keepalive connection. For pools the
    <a href="#quarantine">quarantine</a> is the stronger control.</li>
    <li><strong>The source backport is an option.</strong> For 8.1, rebuilding from the upstream source
    backport into your own image works, but you then own that image's patch stream. Upgrading the branch is
    usually less work.</li>
    <li><strong>Node.</strong> There's no setting that makes Node 20's Brotli decoder safe. The interim control
    is <em>reachability</em>: no untrusted Brotli may reach Kibana (see <a href="#kibana">Protecting
    Kibana</a>).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="guard">New Layer: Request Body Guard (Proposed)</h2>
  <p>
    The reverse proxy already reads every request. It doesn't yet read the <em>structure</em> of request
    bodies. A body guard adds that as a new stage that runs before routing. It's a positive model for bodies,
    in the same spirit as the <a href="/docs/ai-defense/allowlisting#params">parameter shapes</a>: describe what
    a legitimate storefront body looks like, and treat parser-stressing bodies as hostile whatever CVE they're
    for. It follows the project's split: a pure <code>body_guard_rules.lua</code> that can be unit-tested with
    plain <code>lua</code>, and a thin <code>body_guard.lua</code> adapter.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Check</th><th>Default limit</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Request <code>Content-Encoding</code> other than absent/<code>identity</code></td><td>Reject (415)</td><td>No browser compresses form posts. This removes compressed-body parser bugs (Brotli, gzip bombs) for every backend</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>multipart/form-data</code> on a route not on the upload list</td><td>Quarantine</td><td>Every known PHP2Shell trigger is multipart. A storefront takes uploads on a handful of routes at most</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Number of parts / form fields</td><td>≤ 50</td><td>Hash-table pressure during parsing</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Field-name length / bracket depth (<code>a[b][c]…</code>)</td><td>≤ 128 bytes / ≤ 4 levels</td><td>Nested arrays are how 109618 reaches <code>memory_limit</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Field named <code>PHP_SESSION_UPLOAD_PROGRESS</code> or <code>PHPSESSID</code></td><td>Quarantine</td><td>The 109619 upload-progress path. No storefront form sends these</td></tr>
    <tr><td>NUL or control bytes in a part's headers or field name</td><td>Quarantine</td><td>Malformed boundaries and part headers are parser probes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Upload <code>filename</code> containing <code>&lt;?php</code> or <code>|O:</code></td><td>Quarantine</td><td>PHP code or serialized-object payloads in a name field</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Single non-file field larger than 64 KiB</td><td>Score +40</td><td>Large text fields on a storefront are rare, and large fields are used to groom memory</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Body larger than the route's limit</td><td>413</td><td>Today's global <code>client_max_body_size 128M</code> is far more than a storefront needs</td></tr>
  </table>
  <pre><code># nginx.conf (proposed): make the body available to Lua before proxying
client_max_body_size     8m;     # per-location overrides for real upload routes
client_body_buffer_size  1m;     # most bodies stay in memory; larger spill to file
proxy_request_buffering  on;     # already the default; never disable it
client_body_timeout      15s;    # stalled large uploads are a signal, not a customer

access_by_lua_block &#123;
    require("body_guard").check()   -- before router.lua
    ...
&#125;</code></pre>
  <pre><code>-- body_guard_rules.lua (proposed) — pure, no ngx.*, unit-testable
local _M = &#123;&#125;

_M.LIMITS = &#123; max_parts = 50, max_name_len = 128, max_depth = 4,
              max_field_bytes = 65536 &#125;
local POISON = &#123; PHP_SESSION_UPLOAD_PROGRESS = true, PHPSESSID = true &#125;

local function depth(name)
  local _, n = string.gsub(name, "%[", "")
  return n
end

-- Inspect one field name; returns a reason string or nil
function _M.check_name(name, L)
  L = L or _M.LIMITS
  local base = string.match(name, "^[^%[]*")
  if POISON[base] then return "upload_progress_field" end
  if #name > L.max_name_len then return "field_name_too_long" end
  if depth(name) > L.max_depth then return "field_nesting_too_deep" end
  if string.find(name, "[%z\1-\31]") then return "control_bytes_in_name" end
  return nil
end

-- Scan a buffered multipart body: (body, boundary) -> verdict table
function _M.scan_multipart(body, boundary, L)
  L = L or _M.LIMITS
  local parts, delim = 0, "--" .. boundary
  local pos = 1
  while true do
    local s, e = string.find(body, delim, pos, true)
    if not s then break end
    local hdr_end = string.find(body, "\r\n\r\n", e, true)
    if not hdr_end then break end
    parts = parts + 1
    if parts > L.max_parts then
      return &#123; action = "quarantine", reason = "too_many_parts" &#125;
    end
    local hdr = string.sub(body, e + 1, hdr_end)
    local name = string.match(hdr, 'name="([^"]*)"')
    local reason = name and _M.check_name(name, L)
    if reason then return &#123; action = "quarantine", reason = reason &#125; end
    local fname = string.match(hdr, 'filename="([^"]*)"')
    if fname and (string.find(fname, "&lt;?php", 1, true)
                  or string.find(fname, "|O:", 1, true)) then
      return &#123; action = "quarantine", reason = "payload_in_filename" &#125;
    end
    pos = hdr_end + 4
  end
  return &#123; action = "pass", parts = parts &#125;
end

return _M</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Read the buffered body. Don't stream it with <code>resty.upload</code>.</strong> In the access
    phase, <code>resty.upload</code> consumes the request socket, so nothing would be left to proxy. The adapter
    calls <code>ngx.req.read_body()</code>, then scans <code>ngx.req.get_body_data()</code>. If that returns nil
    because the body spilled to a file, it reads <code>ngx.req.get_body_file()</code> in chunks.</li>
    <li><strong>Apply the same depth and count limits to <code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code></strong>
    bodies and to query strings. Nested keys are just as expensive for PHP there.</li>
    <li><strong>Roll it out like the manifest:</strong> <code>observe</code> (log a <code>body_guard</code>
    security event), then enforce once a week of production traffic shows no false positives.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="quarantine">Parser-Exploit Quarantine (Proposed)</h2>
  <p>
    PressHaven's normal response to an attack is to <a href="/docs/ai-defense/allowlisting#divert">divert, not
    block</a>. Parser exploits need an exception, because the decoy is built from the same vulnerable runtime.
    Forwarding a PHP2Shell body to a pool means running a real exploit against real PHP. A
    <strong>quarantine</strong> verdict keeps the deception without the execution:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>The request body is <strong>never forwarded to any PHP backend</strong>, production or pool.</li>
    <li>The edge returns a plausible response itself, such as the shop's normal page for that route or a
    generic <code>200</code>. The client then can't tell it was caught, so there's still no oracle.</li>
    <li>The full body is written to the SIEM as an evidence event (<code>parser_exploit_quarantined</code>,
    capped and hashed), with the verdict reason.</li>
    <li>The client's IP and device signature get the CVE-match risk score, so its <em>later</em>, ordinary
    requests are diverted to a pool as usual and the rest of its session is still observed.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>This turns "the decoy got popped" into "the exploit was recorded, the attacker was tagged, and nothing
  executed", whatever PHP version the containers run.</p>

  <h2 id="kibana">Protecting Kibana (Node2Shell)</h2>
  <p>
    Kibana is less exposed than the storefront. It runs on the SIEM host, behind the firewall, and only analysts
    should reach it. Whether its HTTP server decodes <code>Content-Encoding: br</code> request bodies hasn't been
    verified for 8.17.3, so assume it might. Until <code>STACK_VERSION</code> is upgraded:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Don't expose the published port.</strong> Bind <code>KIBANA_PORT</code> to the analyst VPN or
    loopback interface (<code>"127.0.0.1:$&#123;KIBANA_PORT&#125;:$&#123;KIBANA_PORT&#125;"</code>) instead
    of all interfaces, and reach it over a tunnel.</li>
    <li><strong>Put a reverse proxy in front.</strong> Put Kibana behind a small nginx/OpenResty that rejects any
    request with a <code>Content-Encoding</code> header (<code>if ($http_content_encoding) &#123; return 415;
    &#125;</code>). Browsers never compress request bodies to Kibana, so nothing legitimate breaks. Combine it
    with the <code>ip_whitelist</code> ranges you already trust.</li>
    <li><strong>Limit Kibana's outbound traffic.</strong> Brotli can also arrive in <em>responses</em> Kibana
    fetches. Allow it to reach Elasticsearch only, and turn off news-feed and telemetry fetches, so no untrusted
    server can send it a compressed reply.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="detect">Detection Signals</h2>
  <p>PHP2Shell can succeed with an HTTP 200, so watch the host and the body, not just status codes. Each of these
  is a candidate Kibana alert:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Signal</th><th>Source</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Allowed memory size … exhausted … Unknown on line 0</code></td><td>Apache/PHP error log</td><td>Memory ran out during parsing, before any script ran. That is the 109618 trigger, attempted</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>child pid … exit signal Segmentation fault</code></td><td>Apache error log</td><td>Heap corruption, a failed or partly successful attempt</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>sh</code>/<code>bash</code>/<code>curl</code> spawned by an <code>apache2</code> process</td><td>Container process audit</td><td>Successful RCE. On a pool this is a capture; on production it's an incident</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>body_guard</code> / <code>parser_exploit_quarantined</code> events</td><td>Edge (proposed)</td><td>Attempts, with the full body for analysis</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Unexpected <code>Set-Cookie: PHPSESSID</code> from the storefront</td><td>Edge response headers</td><td>WordPress doesn't use PHP sessions, so a session cookie points at the upload-progress path</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Large uploads that stall until <code>client_body_timeout</code></td><td>nginx access log</td><td>Memory-grooming or timing attempts</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Any request with <code>Content-Encoding: br</code></td><td>Edge, Kibana proxy</td><td>Node2Shell probing (legitimate clients don't send it)</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Vector already ships the edge logs and security events to the SIEM. The Apache error log and process
  events of the WordPress containers need to be added as sources for the host-side rows.</p>

  <h2 id="supply">Supply-Chain Lessons</h2>
  <p>The other Black October incidents don't touch PressHaven's code, but they apply to how it's deployed:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pin images by digest.</strong> The LiteLLM attack shipped through ordinary version tags.
    PressHaven still uses floating tags in places (<code>openresty/openresty:alpine</code>,
    <code>alpine:latest</code>, <code>jasonish/suricata:latest</code>). Pin them by digest, and update the
    digest deliberately through the pool-first path.</li>
    <li><strong>Check what's inside an image, not just its tag.</strong> <code>wordpress:6.8.3</code> sounds
    current while it bundles an end-of-life PHP, and <code>kibana:8.17.3</code> bundles an end-of-life Node. Add
    <code>php -v</code> and <code>node -v</code> checks to your update review.</li>
    <li><strong>Trust stores change too.</strong> After a CA compromise like eMudhra's, rebuilding images
    refreshes their CA bundles. The SIEM's mTLS channel uses its own private CA, so it doesn't depend on the
    public CA set.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="honest">Honest Assessment</h2>
  <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> The edge itself isn't affected and already buffers whole bodies, so a body guard
  can be added without architectural change. The interim PHP settings cover both CVEs without a patched binary.
  Quarantine keeps deception's benefits without running the exploit. The guard's checks describe legitimate
  storefront bodies rather than this one CVE, so they also cover the next parser bug.</p>
  <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> As shipped, production and every pool run vulnerable, end-of-life PHP, and
  Kibana runs end-of-life Node. Until the images are upgraded, the protection is only as good as the interim
  settings, and the body guard and quarantine are proposals, not code. A legitimate upload route on the
  allowlist still passes multipart to PHP, so it relies on the per-part limits. The guard parses bodies the way
  PHP <em>should</em> parse them, and a parser differential between the two could be used to evade it.
  <code>memory_limit=-1</code> trades RCE for possible memory-exhaustion DoS.
  <code>MaxConnectionsPerChild</code> narrows the 109619 window but doesn't close it. None of this replaces
  upgrading.</p>
</DocsLayout>
